{"reasoning": "The pathology report indicates that the patient has perihilar cholangiocarcinoma with a tumor size of 12x12x12 cm, which has invaded the lymphatic vessels and the gallbladder. According to the AJCC Cancer Staging Manual (7th edition), the T category for this patient would be T3, as the tumor has invaded beyond the wall of the bile duct to surrounding adipose tissue and the hepatic parenchyma. The N category cannot be determined solely based on the pathology report, as it requires assessment of regional lymph nodes. However, the report does not mention any regional lymph node metastasis. Therefore, the N category is most likely N0, but it cannot be confirmed without further information. The M category is also not mentioned in the report, and it is assumed to be M0, indicating no distant metastasis.",
"stage": "N0"
}

{"reasoning": "1. The pathology report describes a Grade 3 cholangiocarcinoma in the right segments IV-VIII of the liver, forming a dominant mass (11.5 x 9.2 x 8.6 cm) and multiple (15) other separate nodules within the liver parenchyma. The report also mentions focal angiolymphatic invasion. The T category for this patient would be T3, as the tumor invades adjacent hepatic parenchyma (AJCC 7th edition definition).

2. The report also includes biopsies of the left lobe of the liver (No.1 to No.3), caudate region, peritoneum, gallbladder, and lymph nodes from the hepatic artery. All of these biopsies are negative for tumor. This information indicates that there is no regional lymph node metastasis (N0 category).

3. The AJCC 7th edition does not have an N2 category for cholangiocarcinoma, so the final predicted N stage is N0.", "stage": "N0"}
